<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">here&nbsp;be&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>So the basic premise here is sort of interesting, the whole needing to be known in order to depower something. That's like the opposite of how popularity based entities work, so I like that. But you definitely putting the cart before the horse here, as in you should really flesh out what the actual anomaly is. As a side note this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>My best thought goes to him making the hume level of things really low, as the more people know who he is arguably the more &quot;real&quot; he becomes, and the less people know the less real and so forth.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Seems kinda corny.</p>
<p>The main issue is the lack of narrative. What does that mean? Put simply, it means you need to have a good story to hook the reader. Now no one expects you to have every little detail figured out, but a rough outline of the story you want to tell with your anomaly is expected. Articles need to be more than things that do things nowadays. (What I like to do is decide what I want the reader to feel when reading my article and build it from there.)</p>
<p>Any other questions or if you want me to take a look after changes have been made, you can Wiki-PM me or hit me up on #thecritters in the IRC chat. The chat also has many writers on there to help flesh out your idea, we are a community after all. You also may want to contact another member of the <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/butterfly-squad-roster">Butterfly Squad</a> for further concept critique. Best of luck!</p>
