			<div class="content" id="post-content-4835376">
									

<p>A year later, i have some ideas to develop this further.</p>
<p>The biggest problem is the lack of an actually interesting Anomaly being within the individual, as DrAkimoto pointed out.</p>
<p>As an improvement on the original idea, this anomaly would essentially be a subconscious memetic hazard within the individual. The individual may be a powerful reality bender, or may have simply had an entity take residence within him, and is someone who bases all of their self-worth upon their level of fame. As they didn't naturally achieve this in life, subconsciously the memetic effect slowly started to take effect, where information was replaced with information about the individual.</p>
<p>So essentially, when this person isn't well-known, they slowly take over stored information around them, replacing it with information about him. This is dangerous when applied to normal data such as books and electronic data, as it potentially erases it in entirety. This would be especially problematic as it also effects human memories, and doesn't discriminate, potentially overwriting episodic memory and procedural memory, where individuals forget how to walk and talk due to the overwrite.</p>
<p>This could potentially apply to DNA information, rewriting it into the individual. This wouldn't take that much of an effect, as it would only apply to new cells, but researchers may find themselves growing a different shade of hair. How this would apply to animals is also unknown.</p>
<p>As this occurs when popularity is not achieved, and is a method of achieving said popularity, the foundation could prevent this rather hazardous effect by making the flow of information under their control.</p>

